using System;

namespace Effigy;

/// <summary>
/// Citizen's skeleton, as data.
///
/// WHY A TABLE AND NOT A LOAD. A playermodel is a model that convinces the engine it is citizen -
/// the animations bind by bone NAME against citizen's bind pose, so anything meant to play them
/// has to carry citizen's 95 bones in citizen's hierarchy, posed the way citizen is posed. Reading
/// that out of the compiled model at runtime needs an engine, which puts every test that touches
/// retargeting behind a running editor. As a table it is engine-free like the rest of the kernel,
/// the headless suite can exercise the whole retarget, and the Godot option stays open.
///
/// WHERE THE NUMBERS CAME FROM, since a wrong bind pose here is invisible until something animates.
/// Read off the engine - <see cref="EffigySkeletonImport"/> rebuilds citizen's 95 bones from the
/// compiled `models/citizen/citizen.vmdl` and `effigy_skeleton_probe ... local` dumps each bone's
/// LOCAL basis, which these rows are. The first version came out of a converter that read
/// `citizen.fbx` and changed coordinate systems, and it got every POSITION right while silently
/// getting every ORIENTATION wrong - a transpose at the pelvis, a 180 degree flip on the thighs.
/// Positions are roll-independent, so every check that only compared .Origin passed; the first
/// sign of trouble was a playermodel that walked into a knot. The orientations are now pinned
/// against the engine's own bind pose (0.02 degrees, see CitizenSkeletonTests), which is the one
/// thing citizen's animations were authored against.
///
/// TWO ROOTS, DELIBERATELY. `pelvis` carries the body; `root_IK` carries the IK targets and aim
/// matrices, which are driven rather than skinned. RigDiagnostics complains about multiple roots
/// and is right to in general - this is the exception, and it is citizen's own arrangement, not a
/// mistake to be tidied up.
/// </summary>
public static class CitizenSkeleton
{
